{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He became the Count of Tripoli after the death of his father in 1152. \n*Early Life* \nRaymond III was born around 1140 to Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem his mother was the daughter of King Baldwin II of Jerusalem and his wife Morphia of Melitene. Raymond III was a member of the House of Toulouse and he played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n*Reign* \nRaymond III became the Count of Tripoli in 1152 after the death of his father he was around 12 years old at that time. His mother Hodierna of Jerusalem acted as the regent until he came of age. Raymond III faced many challenges during his reign including the threat from the Muslim states and the struggle for power with the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n*Relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem* \nRaymond III had a complex relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem he was a vassal of the Kingdom but he also wanted to maintain his independence. He married Hodierna's niece which strengthened his ties with the royal family of Jerusalem. Raymond III played an important role in the Battle of Inab in 1149 where the Crusader forces were defeated by the Zengid army. He also participated in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where the Crusader forces led by King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem defeated the Ayyubid army. \n*Later Life and Death* \nRaymond III was captured by Saladin in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin he was later released but he died soon after in 1187. The County of Tripoli was inherited by his godson Raymond of Antioch who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III was remembered as a brave and chivalrous knight who fought for the Crusader states and maintained the independence of the County of Tripoli. \n*Legacy* \nRaymond III left a lasting legacy in the history of the Crusader states he was a skilled military leader and a shrewd politician who navigated the complex web of alliances and rivalries in the region. His reign marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli which was eventually absorbed into the Kingdom of Jerusalem.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -9.775113539944869e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0009117737063206732}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.00015948931104503572}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.5847842693328857}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.4840473532676697}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.887569048150908e-06}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.05688214302062988}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.933898824470816e-06}, {"token": "115", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.006717361975461245}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -6.9141146923357155e-06}, {"token": "118", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.15241791307926178}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.01152948196977377}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.08933473378419876}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.03225897625088692}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -7.271740287251305e-06}, {"token": " II", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0016480210470035672}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " H", "logprob":